values
  ('f940961b-eeb1-5233-82dc-91197af33532', 'c84c9148-846d-58eb-aac1-cd75c49ad306', 'Army', 'enlisted', '42T', null, 'development', 'E-1..E-4', null, 'Private - Specialist/Corporal — recommended career management (applies to every MOS in this career management field)', 'Chapter 3. Recommended career management self-development by rank', 'Not applicable. Soldiers may enter CMF 79 at the rank of Sergeant (SGT) to Military Occupation Specialty (MOS) 42T. However, the Army Force Structure for MOS, 79S and 79T (Title 32) begins at Staff Sergeant (SSG). Force structure for MOS, 79T (Title 10) and 79V begins at SFC.
b. All Ranks: Sergeant - Sergeant Major/Command Sergeant Major.
(1) The Service members Opportunity Colleges Army Degree website provides information on degree programs that relate to military occupational specialties. NCOs who consider lifelong learning as part of self-improvement should attend college to further their self-development.
(2) The Army Learning Management System provides excellent educational advancements in continuing education, leadership, and technical proficiency. The Army Continuing Education System website also lists educational opportunities. The Credentialing Opportunities On-Line website provides a listing of certifications. Visit the Army Education Center on your installation for information on these and other educational programs.', '4bd276ffae50c5ec17e008f3f6b4f40bb626f543a4411010793179662e6b1e75', 2, 2, null, 0, null, null, false, null),
  ('e12780e7-d091-5aeb-ba6b-401b2d916a37', 'c84c9148-846d-58eb-aac1-cd75c49ad306', 'Army', 'enlisted', '42T', null, 'development', 'E-5', null, 'Sergeant — recommended career management (applies to every MOS in this career management field)', 'Chapter 3. Recommended career management self-development by rank', '(1) NCOs will read and demonstrate knowledge of the following military publications: AR 601-210; Army Doctrine Publication (ADP) 3-0; Army Doctrine Reference Publication (ADRP) 3-0; ADP 6-0; ADRP 6-0; ADP 6-22; ADRP 6-22; Training Circular (TC) 7-22.7; FM7-22 as well as all appropriate level maintenance manuals relating to their equipment, battle drills, and current assignment.
(2) Self-development reading suggestions include: The Story of the NCO Corps, David W. Hogan (USA Center of Military History); My Men are Heros by Brad Kasel; Common Sense Training by Arthur S. Collins; Small Unit Leadership by Mike Malone and other Army Leadership publications.
(3) Self-development educational recommendation. Post secondary education enables Soldiers to develop skills and occupational needs, as well as increase their career potential, job satisfaction and educational growth Achievements such as pursuing a college education at this level is not mandatory but places an individual ahead of their peers. Soldiers should also consider entering a program to gain nationally recognized credentials in an appropriate technical discipline; a list of certifications is available on the COOL Web site.
(4) Self-development competitive Soldier boards, such as NCO of the Quarter/Year and the SGT Audie Murphy/SGT Morales Clubs broaden the knowledge base, instill discipline, and improve the Soldier’s ability to communicate verbally.
(5) All NCOs in MOS (79S and 79T) must request an Exception to Policy (ETP), IAW AR 350-1, to be awarded constructive credit for ALC.', '193a50b4b4c04092ec1257df1e72172e1b6f582a97626c40001a556acc071c06', 2, 3, null, 1, null, null, false, null),
  ('9ee3a611-d5ed-5933-ba59-bc8ea09c244c', 'c84c9148-846d-58eb-aac1-cd75c49ad306', 'Army', 'enlisted', '42T', null, 'development', 'E-6', null, 'Staff Sergeant — recommended career management (applies to every MOS in this career management field)', 'Chapter 3. Recommended career management self-development by rank', '(1) NCOs will study and demonstrate proficient knowledge of all previous military publications and self-development recommendations at the SGT level, in addition to the following publications: AR 350-1; AR 623-3; AR 670-1; DA PAM 600-25 and DA PAM 623-3.
(2) Self-development reading suggestions at this level include: readings on famous military leaders (that is, Grant, Pershing, Patton, Bradley, Ridgeway, Westmoreland and Schwartzkopf); The Five Levels of Leadership, John C. Maxwell.
(3) Self-development educational recommendation. Post secondary education enables Soldiers to develop personal, professional, and occupational skills, as well as increase their career potential, job satisfaction and educational growth. Achievement of an associate’s degree (or 60 semester hours of college) at this level places an individual ahead of their peers. Soldiers should also consider entering a program to gain nationally recognized credentials in an appropriate technical discipline; a list of certifications is available on the COOL Web site.
(4) Self-Development competitive Soldier boards, such as NCO of the Quarter/Year and the SGT Audie Murphy/SGT Morales Clubs broaden the knowledge base, instill discipline, and improve the Soldier’s ability to communicate verbally.', '7653b56d2f08bb1c40e6da9710438fba94b545430f318ca342371fbe45e2c90d', 3, 3, null, 2, null, null, false, null),
  ('b21ff725-31cb-5883-a0fe-89014e53b1e6', 'c84c9148-846d-58eb-aac1-cd75c49ad306', 'Army', 'enlisted', '42T', null, 'development', 'E-7', null, 'Sergeant First Class — recommended career management (applies to every MOS in this career management field)', 'Chapter 3. Recommended career management self-development by rank', '(1) NCOs will study and demonstrate expert knowledge to all previous military publications and self-development recommendations at the SSG level, in addition to the following publications: AR 25-50; AR 135-18; AR 750-1; ADP 7-0 and ADRP 7-0.
(2) Self-development reading suggestions include: The Art of War by Sun Tzu (edited by Jomini, Antoine and Messenger, Charles); The One Minute Manager, Kenneth Blanchard; The Resilience Factor, Karen Reivich; Virtual Leadership by Jaclyn Kostner; Extreme Ownership by Jocko Willnik and Leif Babin and continue reading about famous military leaders.
(3) Self-development educational recommendation. Post secondary education enables Soldiers to develop skills and occupational needs, as well as increase their career potential, job satisfaction and educational growth. Achievement of an associate’s degree and an additional 30 semester hours towards a bachelor’s degree (or 90 semester hours toward a bachelor’s degree) places an individual ahead of their peers. Soldiers should also consider entering a program to gain nationally recognized credentials in an appropriate technical discipline; a list of certifications are available on the COOL Web site.
(4) Self-development competitive Soldier boards, such as NCO of the Quarter/Year and the SGT Audie Murphy/SGT Morales Clubs broaden the knowledge base, instill discipline, and improve the Soldier’s ability to communicate verbally.', '545ac37db31dc46f6389400660fb1f51705868c4e61792094cd19a46c3852b68', 3, 4, null, 3, null, null, false, null),
  ('8206e85f-6c76-5f02-ae6a-a0cfafdfeaf6', 'c84c9148-846d-58eb-aac1-cd75c49ad306', 'Army', 'enlisted', '42T', null, 'development', 'E-8', null, 'Master Sergeant/First Sergeant — recommended career management (applies to every MOS in this career management field)', 'Chapter 3. Recommended career management self-development by rank', '(1) As NCOs become more senior in rank, self-motivated development becomes more important; military records are fiercely competitive, and demand increased prerequisites for promotion.
(2) NCOs will study and demonstrate expert knowledge to all previous military publications and self-development recommendations at the SFC level, in addition to the following publications: FM 5-0; AR 220-1; AR 600-8-19, AR 600-20; AR 600-100; DA PAM 611-21; DA PAM 600-25; and FM 7-22; STP 21–24 SMCT. Continuous professional reading aids the Senior NCO in developing and refining organizational leadership skills to coach, teach, and mentor Soldiers. NCOs will broaden their focus and pursue functional course offerings from various sources. Additional self-development reading suggestions include On War and Leadership, Owen Connelly; Medal of Honor; One Man’s Journey from Poverty and Prejudice, Roy P. Benavidez; Sergeant Major of the Army (SMA) Reading List; Extreme Ownership by Jocko Willnik and Leif Babin; Beyond Band of Brothers: The War Memoirs of Major Dick Winters by Dick Winters; Switch – how to change things when change is hard, Dan and Chip Heath. Additionally, NCOs should be familiar with and understand how the Army runs to influence and improve the Army’s systems and contribute to the success of their organization.
(3) Self-Development educational recommendation. Post secondary education enables Soldiers to develop skills and occupational needs, as well as increase their career potential, job satisfaction and educational growth Completion of a bachelor’s degree places an individual ahead of their peers. Soldiers should also consider entering a program to gain nationally recognized credentials in an appropriate technical discipline; a list of certifications are available on the COOL Web site.', 'c41c30cb90a45e6d89f7f2c22d98b6113c2ff9cb45de58caae50b9de2c23c439', 4, 4, null, 4, null, null, false, null),
  ('168cb211-4bab-597a-9850-98f17a100290', 'c84c9148-846d-58eb-aac1-cd75c49ad306', 'Army', 'enlisted', '42T', null, 'development', 'E-9', null, 'Sergeant Major/Command Sergeant Major — recommended career management (applies to every MOS in this career management field)', 'Chapter 3. Recommended career management self-development by rank', '(1) SGM’s/CSM’s often represent the command or Army in civic functions. Therefore, community and public relations skills, and outstanding communication skills are extremely important.
(2) NCOs will study and demonstrate expert knowledge to all previous military publications and self-development recommendations at the MSG level, in addition to the following publications: ADP 5-0; ATP 5-0.1; FM 5-0; FM 6-0; JP 5-0.
(3) Self-Development reading suggestions: The SGM/CSM reads publications on their chains of command professional reading list, Chief of Staff (CSA) of the Army Reading List, SMA Reading list; General Army Links, and Army Leadership publications. Continually reading about world politics, geopolitical issues, Army Doctrine, Training Publications and Field Manuals relating to Army Operations updates, and current battle doctrine enhance the knowledge base of the leader.
(4) Self-Development educational recommendation. Post secondary education enables Soldiers to develop skills and occupational needs, as well as increase their career potential, job satisfaction and educational growth At this level, the pursuit of a master’s degree or higher-level education is desirable. Soldiers should also consider entering a program to gain nationally recognized credentials in an appropriate technical discipline; a list of certifications are available on the COOL Web site.
(5) Self-Development competitive Soldier boards. N/A', 'ce91fe01b50ae75ef1b7be9044b913a9cde53d45bf2299e78d51db25162192ec', 4, 5, null, 5, null, null, false, null),
  ('571337b5-8f3a-5617-9db6-1a42f69aad75', 'c84c9148-846d-58eb-aac1-cd75c49ad306', 'Army', 'enlisted', '79S', null, 'development', 'E-1..E-4', null, 'Private - Specialist/Corporal — recommended career management (applies to every MOS in this career management field)', 'Chapter 3. Recommended career management self-development by rank', 'Not applicable. Soldiers may enter CMF 79 at the rank of Sergeant (SGT) to Military Occupation Specialty (MOS) 42T. However, the Army Force Structure for MOS, 79S and 79T (Title 32) begins at Staff Sergeant (SSG). Force structure for MOS, 79T (Title 10) and 79V begins at SFC.
