  ('2046925c-1a96-5cec-86b6-71a8fcdb04e4', 'c84c9148-846d-58eb-aac1-cd75c49ad306', 'Army', 'enlisted', '42T', null, 'development', null, null, 'Goals for development', 'Chapter 4. MOS 42T Talent Acquisition Specialist', 'The Talent Acquisition Specialists Professional Development Model (PDM) informs the career Talent Acquisition Specialists how their career and professional development patterns should unfold. NCOs gain new perspectives, sharpen skills, garner new insight, and bring fresh objectivity to an organization through a variety of assignments. Talent Acquisition Specialists develop into professional NCOs through assignments centered on leadership positions at the recruiting station and company level. NCOs will make every effort to seek the more challenging and professionally rewarding leadership positions (e.g. Station Commander and First Sergeant (1SG)). NCOs will serve in a variety of challenging positions throughout their career as they progress to develop tactical and technical expertise. These positions (or assignments) are separated into the following categories: operational/institutional (leadership) assignments, and special (key developmental and broadening) assignments. For this chapter, MOS 42T defines operational assignments as a critical or key leadership position. To enhance serious consideration for promotion, the 42T PDM requires successful completion of an operational assignment. NCOs who perform successfully in an operational assignment at a skill level currently above their own, will be given credit for their operational time at the advanced level for which they served. (Example. A SSG who served in an authorized SFC TDA position will be given operational assignment credit as an SFC. A SFC who served in an authorized 1SG TDA position will be given operational assignment credit as a 1SG. MSGs serving in an authorized SGM TDA position will be given operational assignment credit as an SGM). However, to the greatest extent possible all NCOs should serve in a duty position congruent to their current rank and only in exceptional cases should they be assigned to an escalated duty position. Curtailments are highly discouraged during the initial operational assignment. Operational assignments are typically 36 months. Special assignments (or proponent specific challenging/high risk assignments) are further separated into two additional sub-categories; key developmental assignments and broadening assignments. MOS 42T defines a key developmental assignment as positions required to develop critical technical skills and experience. MOS 42T defines a broadening assignment as the purposeful expansion of a NCO’s core MOS proficiency and leadership, provided through developmental assignments, education, training, and other opportunities both within and outside their career management field, resulting in agile and adaptive leaders capable of operating in complex environments. Both key developmental and broadening assignments help develop an NCOs professional knowledge and results in a leader who can operate in complex environments. NCOs should seek key developmental or broadening assignments only after successful completion of an operational assignment. Key developmental or broadening assignment tour lengths are typically 36 months. Key developmental or broadening assignments should not exceed 36 months. NCOs should not seek consecutive key developmental or broadening assignments. NCOs who have demonstrated success in multiple operational assignments, or an operational and key developmental assignment, or an operational and broadening assignment are considered most qualified for promotion. Therefore, the subsequent assignment following an operational assignment will be considered equally weighted regardless if that assignment is a second operational assignment, a key developmental assignment, or a broadening assignment. Serving in a variety of positions is paramount and the experiences gained, helps to develop well- rounded NCOs. However, every Soldier may not have the opportunity to serve in a key developmental or broadening assignment prior to advancing to the next skill level due to availability. Therefore, an NCOs overall performance (not assigned duty position) is the critical factor for promotion to the next pay grade.', 'bc778767b35f2c6c1433e6f1ed89e5c56d221319ca649e6e545d20334e3c21f1', 6, 7, null, 24, null, null, false, null),
  ('56e5c151-dc81-529d-83bd-6249a43f78ba', 'c84c9148-846d-58eb-aac1-cd75c49ad306', 'Army', 'enlisted', '42T', null, 'training', 'E-5', null, 'Sergeant — Institutional training', 'Chapter 4. MOS 42T Talent Acquisition Specialist', 'Talent Acquisition Specialist Qualification Course. Consider NCOs most qualified for promotion if they achieve distinguished recognition such as Honor Graduate and or Distinguished Leader Award, and highly qualified if the achieve the Commandant’s List.', '33c5ea31d188667f33c0923f69e72b99d7aa0cf08109882dce7fb70ba8a3b05e', 7, 7, null, 25, null, null, false, null),
  ('f8954522-c6c4-5349-890d-47278d6617f5', 'c84c9148-846d-58eb-aac1-cd75c49ad306', 'Army', 'enlisted', '42T', null, 'assignments', 'E-5', null, 'Sergeant — Operational assignments', 'Chapter 4. MOS 42T Talent Acquisition Specialist', 'There are very few positions afforded to SGTs. To enhance serious consideration for promotion, the 42T PDM requires successful completion of an operational assignment. However, the 42T structure starts at SSG. Therefore, although SGTs have been allowed to enter the MOS, there is no expectation that they will serve in Station Commander positions. This practice is highly discouraged as it fails to allow the Soldier’s core Talent Acquisition Specialist skills to manifest without additional requirements. The focus during this phase of a career must be on continuing the development and refinement of leadership skills, and tactical and technical expertise. The following duty descriptions are for operational assignments:
i. Talent Acquisition Specialist. These NCOs tactically acquire potential applicants, determine enlistment eligibility, counsel applicants on enlistment programs and options, prepare enlistment applications and process qualified applicants to enlist in the RA and AR. They account for and prepare Future Soldiers for initial entry training by implementing and conducting Army awareness programs throughout an area covering several square miles. These areas may range from sparsely populated rural areas to densely populated urban areas. Talent Acquisition Specialists maintain a network of influencers to include parents, educators and community officials in high schools and colleges. They may also be responsible for several thousand dollars’ worth of government equipment.', '90a195f7677b37e52ed694f4acfede4f2ab9252f36464c57cb0a20a73dd92519', 7, 7, null, 26, null, null, false, null),
  ('1e66a8c1-b39b-50aa-9742-473aaffeca11', 'c84c9148-846d-58eb-aac1-cd75c49ad306', 'Army', 'enlisted', '42T', null, 'self_development', 'E-5', null, 'Sergeant — Self-development', 'Chapter 4. MOS 42T Talent Acquisition Specialist', 'i. Self-development reading suggestions: NCOs will study and demonstrate proficient knowledge of DA PAM 600-25, Chapter 3c (1), in addition to the following publications: UR 601-210; UM 3-0; UM 3-31; UM 3 (VI); and all USAREC TCs. Additional self-development reading suggestions include: Leading From the Front: Answers for the Challenges Leaders Face, David Garic. ii. Self-development education recommendation. Post secondary education enables NCOs to develop skills and occupational needs, as well as increase their career potential, job satisfaction and educational growth. Achievements such as pursuing a college education at this level places an individual ahead of their peers. iii. Self-development competitive Soldier boards. NCOs who receive distinguished recognition such as Talent Acquisition Specialist of the Year, NCO of the Year (Brigade level or higher), SGT Audie Murphy Club, SGT Morales Club, or any installation/post level recognition, etc. clearly develop themselves at a level above their peers. iv. NCOs will remain physically fit and always present an exceptional appearance. Those who score a 540 on the ACFT w/min 80 in each event or those with profiles score 90 points or above in each graded event are clearly ahead of their peers and should be considered most qualified for promotion. NCOs who score 480 on the ACFT w/min 80 in each event or those with profiles score 80 points or above in each graded event are considered highly qualified for promotion.
v. NCOs who have earned the Gold Recruiter Badge “and” the Master Recruiter Badge, or the Gold Recruiter Ring, or the Glen E. Morrell award should be considered most qualified and ahead of their peers when competing for promotion. NCOs who have earned the Gold Recruiter Badge “or” the Master Recruiter Badge should be considered highly qualified when competing for promotion', 'c3ac0b7c67b939d27c631b1c6ec6e7cacddba1eb935395a6c16e7a214fdd5a8b', 7, 8, null, 27, null, null, false, null),
  ('c9ecaf84-eab2-5272-9396-84a519bf69d5', 'c84c9148-846d-58eb-aac1-cd75c49ad306', 'Army', 'enlisted', '42T', null, 'training', 'E-5', null, 'Sergeant — Additional training', 'Chapter 4. MOS 42T Talent Acquisition Specialist', 'None.', '903ea500e7ccf842c1312b6c2f964e3e98ff146456cecec717e9a0e122bf735f', 8, 8, null, 28, null, null, false, null),
  ('94572410-8d97-5d11-97b5-251e9b698d74', 'c84c9148-846d-58eb-aac1-cd75c49ad306', 'Army', 'enlisted', '42T', null, 'assignments', 'E-5', null, 'Sergeant — Special assignments', 'Chapter 4. MOS 42T Talent Acquisition Specialist', 'None.', '903ea500e7ccf842c1312b6c2f964e3e98ff146456cecec717e9a0e122bf735f', 8, 8, null, 29, null, null, false, null),
  ('d7f7d895-f663-5582-b6ec-048ad4ad4463', 'c84c9148-846d-58eb-aac1-cd75c49ad306', 'Army', 'enlisted', '42T', null, 'credentials', 'E-5', null, 'Sergeant — Credentialing Opportunities On Line (COOL) Web site', 'Chapter 4. MOS 42T Talent Acquisition Specialist', 'Seeking industry credentials is an important part of staying competitive within the MOS not only for documented continuing education and development, but to also stay current with industry level technical skills and trends.', '2d4ebc1b3a70f03841c6666046e7d159df6e850998b8957213523e495dab4010', 8, 8, null, 30, null, null, false, null),
  ('c11f9141-9857-5523-82eb-eec311a5e2ea', 'c84c9148-846d-58eb-aac1-cd75c49ad306', 'Army', 'enlisted', '42T', null, 'training', 'E-6', null, 'Staff Sergeant (RA) and Active Guard Reserve (AGR) — Institutional training', 'Chapter 4. MOS 42T Talent Acquisition Specialist', 'ARC, Station Commander Course (SCC / ASI V6), ALC, Consider NCOs most qualified for promotion if they achieve distinguished recognition such as Honor Graduate and or Distinguished Leader Award, and highly qualified if they achieve the Commandant’s List.', '6138b5730e6038a025c7a0ffab09cad0d3aa4f8025b1bb52aee77a4dccbcfdbd', 8, 8, null, 31, null, null, false, null),
  ('0527f5fa-5274-52ad-913b-a77ca993e4b9', 'c84c9148-846d-58eb-aac1-cd75c49ad306', 'Army', 'enlisted', '42T', null, 'assignments', 'E-6', null, 'Staff Sergeant (RA) and Active Guard Reserve (AGR) — Operational assignments', 'Chapter 4. MOS 42T Talent Acquisition Specialist', 'There are few positions afforded to SSGs. To enhance serious consideration for promotion, the 42T PDM requires successful completion of an operational assignment. The focus during this phase of a career must be on continuing the development and refinement of leadership skills, and tactical and technical expertise. The NCO becomes grounded in solid doctrinal procedures and builds a strong technical and tactical foundation for future assignments within United States Army Recruiting Command (USAREC) through career progression assignments. The SSG is responsible for gathering intelligence, building enduring community-centric networks, identifying recruiting targets within their environment, and leveraging technology to complement and enable their leader skills. They decisively tell The Army Story and their personal story to acquire human capital for the Army. Additionally, SSGs, who perform successfully in skill level 4 positions, are also considered most qualified for promotion. The following duty descriptions are for operational assignments:
i. Station Commander. (NPS, AMEDD, SORB and Chaplain) NCOs direct and lead Army recruiting stations in geographically dispersed areas with diverse populations. They supervise NCOs and are responsible for their professional development, morale, health, and welfare. They account for and train Future Soldiers awaiting entry onto active duty and maintain relationships with high schools, colleges, and community partners in the local community. They must maintain control and accountability of equipment and all sensitive items and are responsible for the station achieving its assigned recruiting objectives. ii. Talent Acquisition Specialist. (NPS) These NCOs recruit potential applicants, determine enlistment eligibility, counsel applicants on enlistment programs and options, prepare enlistment applications and process qualified applicants to enlist in the RA and AR. They account for and prepare Future Soldiers for initial entry training by implementing and conducting Army awareness programs throughout an area covering several square miles. These areas may range from sparsely populated rural areas to densely populated urban areas. Talent Acquisition Specialists maintain a network of influencers to include parents, educators and community officials in high schools and colleges. They may also be responsible for several thousand dollars’ worth of government equipment.', 'fa88503f97252141aae50ca874c82f52cd9318a1474f3aea0b22e9a7668ec375', 9, 9, null, 32, null, null, false, null),
  ('b2557121-658a-55ad-86b0-b6af49afcdfc', 'c84c9148-846d-58eb-aac1-cd75c49ad306', 'Army', 'enlisted', '42T', null, 'self_development', 'E-6', null, 'Staff Sergeant (RA) and Active Guard Reserve (AGR) — Self-development', 'Chapter 4. MOS 42T Talent Acquisition Specialist', 'i. Self-development reading suggestions: NCOs will study and demonstrate proficient knowledge of DA PAM 600-25, Chapter 3d (1), in addition to the following publications: AR 601-1; UR 350-1; UM 3-0; UM 3-31; UM 3 (VI); and all USAREC TCs. Additional self-development reading suggestions include: Muddy Boots Leadership, John Chapman; The Gift of Valor, Michael Phillips. ii. Self-development education recommendation. Post secondary education enables NCOs to develop skills and occupational needs, as well as increase their career potential, job satisfaction and educational growth. Achievement of an Associate’s Degree (or 60 semester hours of college) at this level should be considered most qualified for promotion. Achievement of 30 to 59 semester hours should be considered highly qualified. iii. Self-development competitive Soldier boards. NCOs who receive distinguished recognition such as Talent Acquisition Specialist or Station Commander of the Year, NCO of the Year (Brigade lever or higher), SGT Audie Murphy Club, SGT Morales Club, or any installation/post level recognition, etc. clearly develop themselves at a level above their peers. iv. NCOs will remain physically fit and always present an exceptional appearance. Those who score a 540 on the ACFT w/min 80 in each event or those with profiles score 90 points or above in each graded event are clearly ahead of their peers and should be considered most qualified for promotion. NCOs who score 480 on the ACFT w/min 80 in each event or those with profiles score 80 points or above in each graded event are considered highly qualified for promotion.
v. NCOs who have earned the Gold Recruiter Badge “and” the Master Recruiter Badge, or the Gold Recruiter Ring, or the Glen E. Morrell award should be considered most qualified and ahead of their peers when competing for promotion. NCOs who have earned the Gold Recruiter Badge “or” the Master Recruiter Badge should be considered highly qualified when competing for promotion.', 'af5f18f43e2183786da028655667b120bdaae882e159f64cedb4656e227b065e', 9, 10, null, 33, null, null, false, null),
  ('0035fb24-c857-5c31-91af-68ed5ec725a8', 'c84c9148-846d-58eb-aac1-cd75c49ad306', 'Army', 'enlisted', '42T', null, 'training', 'E-6', null, 'Staff Sergeant (RA) and Active Guard Reserve (AGR) — Additional training', 'Chapter 4. MOS 42T Talent Acquisition Specialist', 'None.', '903ea500e7ccf842c1312b6c2f964e3e98ff146456cecec717e9a0e122bf735f', 10, 10, null, 34, null, null, false, null),
  ('7d4c5ef3-a290-5d7d-9be7-095416387a4e', 'c84c9148-846d-58eb-aac1-cd75c49ad306', 'Army', 'enlisted', '42T', null, 'assignments', 'E-6', null, 'Staff Sergeant (RA) and Active Guard Reserve (AGR) — Special assignments', 'Chapter 4. MOS 42T Talent Acquisition Specialist', 'None.', '903ea500e7ccf842c1312b6c2f964e3e98ff146456cecec717e9a0e122bf735f', 10, 10, null, 35, null, null, false, null),
  ('edebdb43-8f9d-50f2-a096-1b63ab18b503', 'c84c9148-846d-58eb-aac1-cd75c49ad306', 'Army', 'enlisted', '42T', null, 'development', 'E-6', null, 'Staff Sergeant (RA) and Active Guard Reserve (AGR) — COOL Web site', 'Chapter 4. MOS 42T Talent Acquisition Specialist', 'Seeking industry credentials is an important part of staying competitive within the MOS not only for documented continuing education and development, but to also stay current with industry level technical skills and trends. Earning the Society for Human Resources-Certified Professional (SHRM-CP) credential makes you most qualified for promotion. Completed credentials (i.e. USMAP, Lean Six Sigma, etc.), makes you highly qualified for promotion.', '68d22bdc8ca92574c54cf3016a61fffd017a852b23f61d08fd3dadbce2d30a53', 10, 10, null, 36, null, null, false, null),
  ('3b16d4a3-1f89-526a-a3e8-f5dba724c28c', 'c84c9148-846d-58eb-aac1-cd75c49ad306', 'Army', 'enlisted', '42T', null, 'training', 'E-7', null, 'Sergeant First Class (RA and AGR) — Institutional training', 'Chapter 4. MOS 42T Talent Acquisition Specialist', 'ARC, SCC (ASI V6), SLC. Consider NCOs most qualified for promotion if they achieve distinguished recognition such as Honor Graduate and or Distinguished Leader Award and highly qualified if they achieve the Commandant’s List.', '8d678d7db58ec89cd3b6905d815d19a00ebda59240cd4759e07590a592df85af', 10, 10, null, 37, null, null, false, null),
  ('a9d9de32-5e0f-5139-9290-80f9b44cd16f', 'c84c9148-846d-58eb-aac1-cd75c49ad306', 'Army', 'enlisted', '42T', null, 'assignments', 'E-7', null, 'Sergeant First Class (RA and AGR) — Operational Assignments', 'Chapter 4. MOS 42T Talent Acquisition Specialist', 'There is no substitute for serving in key leadership positions. It is recommended that at the time of the evaluation board the most qualified NCOs have completed 36 months of successful Station Commander time. Additionally, SFCs, who perform successfully in skill level 5 positions, are also considered most qualified for promotion. To enhance serious consideration for promotion, the 42T PDM requires successful completion of an operational assignment, typically 36 months. The following duty description is for operational assignments:
i. Station Commander. (NPS, AMEDD, SORB and Chaplain) NCOs direct and lead Army recruiting stations in geographically dispersed areas with diverse populations. They supervise NCOs and are responsible for their professional development, morale, health, and welfare. They account for and train Future Soldiers awaiting entry onto active duty and maintain relationships with high schools, colleges, and community partners in the local community. They must maintain control and accountability of equipment and all sensitive items and are responsible for the station achieving its assigned recruiting objectives.', 'd77ffb61f666510ca9fbaa2007b6a6e113010f432ed17d0ce8d8c48412f12807', 10, 11, null, 38, null, null, false, null),
  ('78c750a7-8330-5d4c-b9e8-0da4daf5cb29', 'c84c9148-846d-58eb-aac1-cd75c49ad306', 'Army', 'enlisted', '42T', null, 'self_development', 'E-7', null, 'Sergeant First Class (RA and AGR) — Self-development', 'Chapter 4. MOS 42T Talent Acquisition Specialist', 'i. Self-development reading suggestions: NCOs will study and demonstrate proficient knowledge of DA PAM 600-25, Chapter 3e (1), UM 3-30; and all USAREC TCs. Additional self-development reading suggestions include: Moral Issues in Military Decision Making, Anthony E. Hartle; We Were Soldiers Once…and Young, Harold G. Moore. vi. Self-development education recommendation. Post secondary education enables NCOs to develop skills and occupational needs, as well as increase their career potential, job satisfaction and educational growth. Achievement of an Associate’s Degree plus an additional 30 semester hours towards a Bachelor’s degree (or 90 semester hours toward a Bachelor’s degree) at this level should be considered most qualified for promotion. Achievement of 60 to 89 semester hours should be considered highly qualified. ii. Self-development competitive Soldier boards. NCOs who receive distinguished recognition such as Station Commander of the Year, NCO of the Year (Brigade level or higher), SGT Audie Murphy Club, SGT Morales Club, or any installation/post level recognition, etc. clearly develop themselves at a level above their peers. iv. NCOs will remain physically fit and always present an exceptional appearance. Those who score a 540 on the ACFT w/min 80 in each event or those with profiles score 90 points or above in each graded event are clearly ahead of their peers and should be considered most qualified for promotion. NCOs who score 480 on the ACFT w/min 80 in each event or those with profiles score 80 points or above in each graded event are considered highly qualified for promotion.
v. NCOs who have earned the Gold Recruiter Badge “and” the Master Recruiter Badge, or the Gold Recruiter Ring, or the Glen E. Morrell award should be considered most qualified and ahead of their peers when competing for promotion. NCOs who have earned the Gold Recruiter Badge “or” the Master Recruiter Badge should be considered highly qualified when competing for promotion.', 'e294372f7574836154e1d27184d13505e9e7d43ce33f59a6586d3a888bc44251', 11, 11, null, 39, null, null, false, null),
  ('7f12a36b-40f2-514c-834a-bd90ffc17665', 'c84c9148-846d-58eb-aac1-cd75c49ad306', 'Army', 'enlisted', '42T', null, 'training', 'E-7', null, 'Sergeant First Class (RA and AGR) — Additional training', 'Chapter 4. MOS 42T Talent Acquisition Specialist', 'Common Faculty Development-Instructor Course, Common Faculty Development -Training Course, Drill SGT Course, Equal Opportunity Course, Guidance Counselor Operations Course (ASI V7), Health Care Recruiter Course (ASI 4N), Master Resiliency Trainer, Sexual Harassment/Assault Response and Prevention (SHARP).', 'cc7c0aac931d92bf8ed31c6fe7ea814471407b77bde7225766e3d3369b407392', 12, 12, null, 40, null, null, false, null),
  ('10b4d139-100c-5239-b41c-775d7ba4dfc2', 'c84c9148-846d-58eb-aac1-cd75c49ad306', 'Army', 'enlisted', '42T', null, 'assignments', 'E-7', null, 'Sergeant First Class (RA and AGR) — Special assignments', 'Chapter 4. MOS 42T Talent Acquisition Specialist', 'Examples of key developmental assignments which prepare a SFC for success as a MSG/1SG include, but not limited to, (in alphabetical order): All USAREC, Brigade, and Battalion Staff positions, AMEDD Recruiting NCO, Assistant Operations NCO, Doctrine NCO, Faculty and Staff Development Division NCOIC, Guidance Counselor, Instructor/Writer, Master Trainer, Operations NCO, Professional Development NCO (G-1), Research and Integration NCO, SORB Recruiter, Training Developer, Training Management, Virtual Recruiter, and Virtual Recruiting Station Commander. During this career stage, the NCO focuses on developing organizational management skills while mastering skill level 4 tasks and strengthening leadership skills. They utilize sensors in their area of operation using Area, Structures, Capabilities, Organizations, People and Events (ASCOPE) to analyze environmental conditions as a part of the mission variables, mission, enemy, terrain and weather, troops and support available, time available and civil considerations (METT- TC). Broadening assignments expand the NCOs leadership capabilities and experiences. Examples of broadening assignments include, but are not limited to, (in alphabetical order): Equal Opportunity (EO) Advisor, and Sexual Assault Response Coordinator (SARC) NCO. NCOs should seek special assignments after successful completion of operational assignments. Special assignments should not exceed 36 months. NCOs should not seek consecutive special assignments.', '4ff230beca2dc80bef052deab21506145954dbcf7e2a730d06e45d89ddbf46d8', 12, 12, null, 41, null, null, false, null),
  ('5d5724f0-3dda-5339-98a9-18ed36afffc5', 'c84c9148-846d-58eb-aac1-cd75c49ad306', 'Army', 'enlisted', '42T', null, 'development', 'E-7', null, 'Sergeant First Class (RA and AGR) — COOL Web site', 'Chapter 4. MOS 42T Talent Acquisition Specialist', 'Seeking industry credentials is an important part of staying competitive within the MOS not only for documented continuing education and development, but to also stay current with industry level technical skills and trends. Earning the SHRM-CP credential makes you most qualified for promotion. Completed credentials (i.e. USMAP, Lean Six Sigma, etc.), makes you highly qualified for promotion.', 'dea7e8a2577d0a5abb4f06fe8c04f6b28d116362b74b5f7540c51e23cbaff16f', 12, 12, null, 42, null, null, false, null),
  ('bdab71a3-218f-57b2-bd0b-397add54f183', 'c84c9148-846d-58eb-aac1-cd75c49ad306', 'Army', 'enlisted', '42T', null, 'training', 'E-8', null, 'Master Sergeant/1SG (RA and AGR) — Institutional training', 'Chapter 4. MOS 42T Talent Acquisition Specialist', 'MLC. Consider NCOs most qualified for promotion if they achieve distinguished recognition such as Honor Graduate and or Distinguished Leader Award, and highly qualified if they achieve the Commandant’s List.', 'a4366ba34d4d61f3faa6f7a027cef2bca308a7d58b2a731ec53894504e3eae8f', 12, 12, null, 43, null, null, false, null),
  ('f2da572f-a35d-5c1a-bd1b-d3b2a9b460b4', 'c84c9148-846d-58eb-aac1-cd75c49ad306', 'Army', 'enlisted', '42T', null, 'assignments', 'E-8', null, 'Master Sergeant/1SG (RA and AGR) — Operational assignments', 'Chapter 4. MOS 42T Talent Acquisition Specialist', 'The single most demanding and critical leadership position for a MSG is 1SG. To enhance serious consideration for promotion to SGM, or selection to CSM, the 42T PDM requires the successful completion of an operational assignment as a 1SG. It is recommended that at the time of the board the most qualified NCOs have completed 24 months of highly successful 1SG time. NCOs who have successfully performed as a 1SG are generally more qualified than those who have not. Additionally, MSGs, who perform successfully in skill level 6 positions, are also considered most qualified for promotion. The following duty descriptions are for operational assignments.
i. 1SG. 1SGs serve as the senior enlisted advisor to the Company Commander. They must be SMEs on all recruiting matters within the recruiting company and serve as the primary source for Soldier career guidance and planning. While being leadership focused, they also must remain technically proficient as they must operate advanced information technology systems that support recruiting efforts. They assist the Company Commander in leading and supervising the recruiting company operations within the guidelines of doctrine and command directives. 1SGs are responsible for training NCOs on current recruiting policies. They lead, supervise, coordinate, and implement daily recruiting activities. They monitor, inspect, and evaluate collective and individual Soldier development training programs. As SMEs, 1SGs must interpret regulations for leaders and subordinates.', '7c7ba48770b29dde5205ab02e9295a6287764f5aa7c78c85a38050aa0581b45b', 12, 13, null, 44, null, null, false, null),
  ('62af8bf3-3766-5067-bfe2-7773a019ee12', 'c84c9148-846d-58eb-aac1-cd75c49ad306', 'Army', 'enlisted', '42T', null, 'self_development', 'E-8', null, 'Master Sergeant/1SG (RA and AGR) — Self-development', 'Chapter 4. MOS 42T Talent Acquisition Specialist', 'i. Self-development reading suggestions: NCOs will study and demonstrate proficient knowledge of DA PAM 600-25, Chapter 3f (1). NCOs will study and master the following publications: All USAREC TCs. Additional self- development reading suggestions include: Band of Brothers, Stephen E. Ambrose; Leadership Secrets of Colin Powell, Owen Harari. ii. Self-development education recommendation. Post secondary education enables NCOs to develop skills and occupational needs, as well as increase their career potential, job satisfaction and educational growth. Completion of a Bachelor’s at this level should be considered most qualified for promotion. Achievement of 90 to 119 semester hours should be considered highly qualified. iii. Self-development competitive Soldier boards. N/A. iv. NCOs will remain physically fit and always present an exceptional appearance. Those who score a 540 on the ACFT w/min 80 in each event or those with profiles score 90 points or above in each graded event are clearly ahead of their peers and should be considered most qualified for promotion. NCOs who score 480 on the ACFT w/min 80 in each event or those with profiles score 80 points or above in each graded event are considered highly qualified for promotion.
v. NCOs who have earned the Gold Recruiter Badge “and” the Master Recruiter Badge, or the Gold Recruiter Ring, or the Glen E. Morrell award should be considered most qualified and ahead of their peers when competing for promotion. NCOs who have earned the Gold Recruiter Badge “or” the Master Recruiter Badge should be considered highly qualified when competing for promotion.', '9c7339516c21c09527cb6058b4c2efb6b6206c8ddcf76e7f414bc068faf3d90a', 13, 14, null, 45, null, null, false, null),
  ('f6508843-5e9b-5809-bb32-ecb8f7c07774', 'c84c9148-846d-58eb-aac1-cd75c49ad306', 'Army', 'enlisted', '42T', null, 'training', 'E-8', null, 'Master Sergeant/1SG (RA and AGR) — Additional training', 'Chapter 4. MOS 42T Talent Acquisition Specialist', 'Department of the Army (DA) Inspector General (IG) Course, Equal Opportunity Course, Guidance Counselor Operations Course, Master Resiliency Trainer Course, SARC, and Training and Education Middle Management Course.', '9c5cdada1314c061929cc8706885463f71e8ad91cb74bae829008f2514e69f96', 14, 14, null, 46, null, null, false, null),
  ('c36f0987-f385-57cc-a2fd-555b51d8d3d6', 'c84c9148-846d-58eb-aac1-cd75c49ad306', 'Army', 'enlisted', '42T', null, 'assignments', 'E-8', null, 'Master Sergeant/1SG (RA and AGR) — Special assignments', 'Chapter 4. MOS 42T Talent Acquisition Specialist', 'Examples of key developmental assignments which prepare a MSG for success as a SGM include, but not limited to, (in alphabetical order): All USAREC, Brigade, and Battalion Staff positions, Instructor/Writer for the Company Commander-1SG Course, Research and Integration NCOIC, RRC Division Chief, Senior Guidance Counselor, Senior Master Trainer, Senior Operations NCO, Senior Professional Development NCO (G-1), Senior Personnel Development NCO, and Training Management NCOIC, NCOs should only seek these assignments after successful completion of an operational assignment. Broadening assignments expand the NCOs leadership capabilities and experiences. Examples of broadening assignments include, but are not limited to, (in alphabetical order): Assistant Inspector General (IG), Equal Opportunity (EO) Advisor, Investigation Team NCOIC, Liaison NCOIC, SARC NCOIC. Special assignments should not exceed 36 months. NCOs should not seek consecutive back-to-back special assignments.', 'ab80fb7fd247b601ac51a23e8876d077c49627bea4ad390f747c2e67e2cf0ffb', 14, 14, null, 47, null, null, false, null),
  ('18f167a3-8491-50a0-a7d4-a7e138d58c81', 'c84c9148-846d-58eb-aac1-cd75c49ad306', 'Army', 'enlisted', '42T', null, 'development', 'E-8', null, 'Master Sergeant/1SG (RA and AGR) — Army Career Degrees', 'Chapter 4. MOS 42T Talent Acquisition Specialist', 'See SOCAD Army Career Degree Program.\', '22f5e92d02a3b03e870f8478651669e573f05df616d369113db86a0d673d1fc3', 14, 14, null, 48, null, null, false, null),
  ('9f923ad5-d9cf-577c-923b-1fd1f87d1aec', 'c84c9148-846d-58eb-aac1-cd75c49ad306', 'Army', 'enlisted', '42T', null, 'development', 'E-8', null, 'Master Sergeant/1SG (RA and AGR) — COOL Web site', 'Chapter 4. MOS 42T Talent Acquisition Specialist', 'Seeking industry credentials is an important part of staying competitive within the MOS not only for documented continuing education and development, but to also stay current with industry level technical skills and trends. Earning the Society for Human Resources-Certified Professional (SHRM-CP) credential makes you most qualified for promotion. Completed credentials (i.e. USMAP, Lean Six Sigma, etc.), makes you highly qualified for promotion.', '68d22bdc8ca92574c54cf3016a61fffd017a852b23f61d08fd3dadbce2d30a53', 14, 14, null, 49, null, null, false, null),
  ('a7dedda8-0a3a-5102-881b-9c50dc014640', 'c84c9148-846d-58eb-aac1-cd75c49ad306', 'Army', 'enlisted', '42T', null, 'training', 'E-9', null, 'Sergeant Major/Command Sergeant Major (RA and AGR) — Institutional training', 'Chapter 4. MOS 42T Talent Acquisition Specialist', 'US Army Sergeants Major Course, Executive Leaders Course.', 'cb58d62a67cbedfd551d320ccd270848faeb799bf56ba71d9705efbc2b33ed22', 14, 14, null, 50, null, null, false, null),
  ('ca4087f3-41b0-5f2a-83fb-dec6d6c12285', 'c84c9148-846d-58eb-aac1-cd75c49ad306', 'Army', 'enlisted', '42T', null, 'assignments', 'E-9', null, 'Sergeant Major/Command Sergeant Major (RA and AGR) — Operational assignments', 'Chapter 4. MOS 42T Talent Acquisition Specialist', 'NCOs gain experience and leadership skills through a variety of challenging and key developmental duty assignments that are paramount for SGM/CSM. Normally, a Soldier is promoted to SGM, and may then subsequently compete for appointment to CSM. They require excellent communication skills and the ability to lead using mission orders to enable disciplined initiative within the commander’s intent to empower agile and adaptive leaders to conduct operations in a geographically dispersed environment. The continuous high-profile interaction with military leaders, governmental representatives, and civilian community leaders necessitates robust interpersonal skills and an exemplary representation of the Army image. They are the senior NCO of the command and, therefore, the training professional within the unit. They assist the commander by overseeing and driving the entire training and leader development program. While serving in a staff position, they perform as technical experts advising on policy development and regulatory interpretation. The following duty descriptions are for operational assignments.
i. CSM (Battalion/6C). The CSM is the senior enlisted advisor to the commander at the recruiting battalion. The CSM is the senior enlisted trainer and spokesperson that enforces established policies and standards for enlisted NCOs about performance, care, conduct, appearance, effective personnel utilization, management, and training; they also ensure that subordinate NCOs do the same. They assess recruiting station personnel for tactical, technical, interpersonal, and conceptual skills and ensure training occurs when necessary. CSMs assign duties and evaluate the performance of cadre and detailed recruiters in support of recruiting and personnel development. They interact with educators, community partners and civic organizations to leverage support for recruiting activities. They also foster relationships with Army Divisions, MACOMS and Reserve Regional Commands within their assigned geographic boundaries. ii. CSM (Brigade/7C). The CSM is the senior enlisted advisor to the commander at the recruiting brigade. The CSM is the senior enlisted trainer and spokesperson that enforces established policies and standards for enlisted NCOs about performance, care, conduct, appearance, effective personnel utilization and management and training; they also ensure that subordinate NCOs do the same. They assess recruiting company personnel for tactical, technical, interpersonal, and conceptual skills and ensure training occurs when necessary. CSMs assign duties and evaluate the performance of cadre recruiters in support of recruiting and personnel development. They interact with educators, community partners and civic organizations to leverage support for recruiting activities. They also foster relationships with Army Divisions, MACOMS and Reserve Regional Commands within their assigned geographic boundaries. iii. CSM (USAREC/8D). The CSM of USAREC provides advice to the Commanding General (CG) and staff on personnel utilization and management, leader development, recruiting the forces, resourcing the command, adapting doctrine, training, and education for the future force of the command. The CSM strengthens and extends outreach networks, oversees the development and deployment of technological solutions, creates, and enables futures plans and concepts. The CSM works with an annual budget more than 274 million dollars and has direct oversight for the health, welfare, and safety for over 12,500 NCOs and Civilians geographically dispersed throughout the U.S., its territories and OCONUS. iv. Self-development reading suggestions: The SGM/CSM reads publications on their chain of command professional reading lists, CSA Reading List, SMA Reading list; General Army Links, and Army Leadership publications. Continual reading about world politics, geopolitical issues, Army Doctrine, Training Publications and Field Manuals relating to Army Operations and current battle doctrine enhance the knowledge base of the leader.', 'efdba34d8e50733ebf81e4e5faaae07158074da34c68d557f8ff8aec42455c4a', 14, 15, null, 51, null, null, false, null),
  ('74ab21b6-8ec8-58a2-9a1c-aaef9a1fc255', 'c84c9148-846d-58eb-aac1-cd75c49ad306', 'Army', 'enlisted', '42T', null, 'self_development', 'E-9', null, 'Sergeant Major/Command Sergeant Major (RA and AGR) — Self-development', 'Chapter 4. MOS 42T Talent Acquisition Specialist', 'i. Self-development education recommendation. The pursuit of a Master’s Degree is highly encouraged.\ ii. Self-development competitive Soldier boards. N/A.', '1b2892906fd52b3c169d878eae256f03247b3b58a06ca9a8d2a494fd867b4851', 16, 16, null, 52, null, null, false, null),
  ('6c236edb-8757-5e4a-9026-97542baa6b8c', 'c84c9148-846d-58eb-aac1-cd75c49ad306', 'Army', 'enlisted', '42T', null, 'training', 'E-9', null, 'Sergeant Major/Command Sergeant Major (RA and AGR) — Additional training', 'Chapter 4. MOS 42T Talent Acquisition Specialist', 'USAREC Operations SGM Course, Senior Enlisted Joint Professional Military Education, Force Structure and Management Course, and the Pre- Command Course.', 'f067a28e38bb597c99df7f9409da1510337b7f6032c148f97e14ed199cfcd40c', 16, 16, null, 53, null, null, false, null),
  ('03d4791a-3ab9-500a-a38c-249bb7925465', 'c84c9148-846d-58eb-aac1-cd75c49ad306', 'Army', 'enlisted', '42T', null, 'assignments', 'E-9', null, 'Sergeant Major/Command Sergeant Major (RA and AGR) — Special assignments', 'Chapter 4. MOS 42T Talent Acquisition Specialist', 'Key developmental assignments include (but are not limited to):
i. Battalion Operations SGM (6S). They are the senior subject matter expert in the battalion S3 responsible for oversight of all recruiting operations within the battalion. These NCOs serve as the operations and management advisor for 1SGs, Guidance Counselors, and RA and AR Talent Acquisition Specialists. Operations SGMs lead a section responsible for monitoring progress toward recruiting objectives and advise leadership on results of implementation and success of daily recruiting operations such as prospecting, lead generation, and applicant processing. They must review requests for recruiting waivers using existing regulatory guidance and policy and provides recommendations to leaders to ensure applicants meet the Army’s established moral code and physical requirements. ii. Brigade Operations SGM (6X). They are the senior subject matter expert in the brigade S3 responsible for oversight of all recruiting operations within the brigade. They perform market data analysis within the brigade’s assigned area to ensure the Army is the service of choice for America’s youth. Operations SGMs lead a section responsible for monitoring progress toward unit objectives and advise leadership on results of implementation and success of daily recruiting operations. They must review requests for recruiting waivers using existing regulatory guidance and policy and provides recommendations to leaders to ensure applicants meet the Army’s established moral code and physical requirements. iii. USAREC G-3 SGM (7X). Serves as the liaison between HQ’s USAREC and brigades/battalions for interpretation of official policy, regulations and guidance regarding enlistment; responsible for the conduct of inspections and training for subordinate battalion and brigade operations sections and guidance counselor shops; supervises the daily workload of assigned enlisted and civilian employees to successfully meet suspense’s and mission requirements; reviews automated reports, programs and systems to identify operational changes affecting recruiting the force. The USAREC G-3 SGM has typically served as a Recruiting Battalion or Brigade CSM. iv. CMF 79 Proponent SGM (7S). Responsible for the eight life- cycle functions of 79 CMF; life-cycle structure, personnel acquisition and distribution, individual training and education, sustainment, mobilization, professional development, and separation. Advises the command on Army personnel policies and programs that impact MOS 42T, 79S, 79T, and 79V; prepares Centralized Slating List guidance for the USAREC CG and CSM; conducts analysis of the centralized board results; routinely advises the USAREC CSM on enlisted issues pertaining to the health and welfare of the CMF 79. The CMF 79 Proponent SGM has typically served as a Recruiting Battalion or Brigade CSM.
v. RRC Recruiting Department SGM (6S). Performs duties as the senior NCO for all recruiting courses taught at the Recruiting and Retention College. This SGM is responsible for all programs of instruction, instructor certifications in accordance with TRADOC accreditation requirements and synchronization efforts between HQ’s, USAREC, Human Resources Command and DA G-1. The SGM is responsible for providing advice to the Recruiting and Retention College Commandant and CSM regarding policies and procedures related to all training support packages and operational doctrine implementation within the institutional learning environment to bridge the gap between the three training domains (Institutional, Operational, and Self-Development). The Recruiting Department SGM has typically served as a Recruiting Battalion or Brigade CSM. vi. All other USAREC Staff SGM positions vii. Broadening Assignments for a SGM include (but are not limited to): USAREC Inspector General SGM, USAREC Investigation Team SGM, USASMA Instructor, USASMA Fellowship Instructor. viii. NCOs will remain physically fit and always present an exceptional appearance. Those who score a 540 on the ACFT w/min 80 in each event or those with profiles score 90 points or above in each graded event are clearly ahead of their peers and should be considered most qualified for promotion. NCOs who score 480 on the ACFT w/min 80 in each event or those with profiles score 80 points or above in each graded event are considered highly qualified for promotion.', '4df71a928da2fdb2de0f139de6f99f02ea44d83206edda1f7fe25dc4e0d19461', 16, 17, null, 54, null, null, false, null),
  ('c18b9868-9c70-529d-a103-c845d1f9fdf3', 'c84c9148-846d-58eb-aac1-cd75c49ad306', 'Army', 'enlisted', '42T', null, 'development', 'E-9', null, 'Sergeant Major/Command Sergeant Major (RA and AGR) — COOL Web site', 'Chapter 4. MOS 42T Talent Acquisition Specialist', 'Seeking industry credentials is an important part of staying competitive within the MOS not only for documented continuing education and development, but to also stay current with industry level technical skills and trends. Earning the Society for Human Resources-Certified Professional (SHRM-CP) credential, or other completed credentials (i.e. USMAP, Lean Six Sigma, etc.), places you ahead of your peers.', '0bb9c0b57931dca0aa73fb019751dd150dbac6c04a3730ed8bdadc672581d1f1', 17, 17, null, 55, null, null, false, null),
  ('a9914cae-0ded-50da-9301-1d66cac40edf', 'c84c9148-846d-58eb-aac1-cd75c49ad306', 'Army', 'enlisted', '79S', null, 'development', null, null, 'Goals for development', 'Chapter 6. MOS 79S Career Counselor', 'The purpose of the Career Counselor PDM is to educate Career Counselors on how their careers and professional development patterns should unfold. Career Counselors should seek to become well-rounded professionals during their careers by demonstrating their competence within multi- echelon environments, encompassing a variety of assignments, both CONUS and OCONUS. NCOs will serve in several key and developmental positions throughout their career as they progress to develop tactical and technical expertise. These positions (or assignments) are separated into two categories: operational and special. For the purpose of this chapter, MOS 79S defines operational assignments as a critical or key position that directly impacts the Army’s retention mission. MOS 79S defines a key developmental assignment as a position that is required to develop critical technical and leadership skills and experience. MOS 79S defines a broadening assignment as the purposeful expansion of an NCOs core proficiency through assignments both within and outside the MOS while generating the 79S field. To enhance serious consideration for promotion, the 79S PDM requires successful completion of an operational assignment at each skill level such as Career Counselor, Senior Career Counselor, , Senior Retention Operations NCO, Command Career Counselor, or Senior Command Career Counselor. Upon successful completion of an operational assignment, Career Counselors should seek the most challenging, professionally rewarding special assignments, further divided into generating or broadening type positions such as Instructor/Writer, Small Group Leader (NCOA), HRC Analyst, Retention Operations NCO, Trainer/Developer, Senior Army Retention Operations NCO, Senior Career Management NCO, Talent Management NCO, Senior HRC Analyst, Chief Instructor, Retain NCOIC, or Senior Trainer/developer , that provide the greatest potential for advancement. Soldiers should seek special assignments only after successful completion of an operational assignment. NCOs should not seek consecutive special assignments as back-to-back, non- operational assignments should be avoided. Soldiers who have demonstrated success in multiple operational assignments, or an operational and key developmental assignment, or an operational and broadening assignment are considered best qualified for promotion. Serving in a variety of positions is paramount. While the experiences gained help to develop well rounded Soldiers, how well the NCO performs in their assigned duty position is equally (if not more) important. An NCOs overall performance is a critical factor for promotion to the next pay grade.', '8beba98c48d13f2ae69d1e01dee2e9feec8b85b901e4d1ffa0c4a93b3a6f3526', 18, 19, null, 56, null, null, false, null),
  ('1f2e54d9-9ae7-559d-b9bf-765f40044825', 'c84c9148-846d-58eb-aac1-cd75c49ad306', 'Army', 'enlisted', '79S', null, 'training', 'E-6', null, 'Staff Sergeant — Institutional training', 'Chapter 6. MOS 79S Career Counselor', 'Career Counselor Course and Advanced Leader Course (ALC). Consider NCOs ahead of their peers and best qualified for promotion if they achieve distinguished recognition such as Distinguished Honor Graduate, Distinguished Leadership or Commandant’s List.', '1314fafb44c054ef4378c6c943612197f41a39c693673072e4a7ce368431f1df', 19, 19, null, 57, null, null, false, null),
  ('9f0a6653-1d1e-55d2-ac01-ec8467a27469', 'c84c9148-846d-58eb-aac1-cd75c49ad306', 'Army', 'enlisted', '79S', null, 'assignments', 'E-6', null, 'Staff Sergeant — Operational Assignments', 'Chapter 6. MOS 79S Career Counselor', 'The focus during this phase of a Career Counselor''s career must be on continued development and refinement of their counseling and leadership skills in addition to their technical expertise. The Career Counselor''s job as the technical advisor to the Commander is essential to the maintenance of unit combat readiness and Army end strength. They implement local retention program procedures, counsel, and assist Soldiers on career goals and opportunities and train additional duty reenlistment NCOs. Career Counselors who have demonstrated success while serving at least 24 months at the battalion level or higher are considered best qualified for promotion.', '84ea84eef19ab0e63d00a601decde0bc8a2850babbe01aa39d3016724e105bdf', 19, 20, null, 58, null, null, false, null),
  ('2b369399-3d71-5920-aaac-cf5796b82bc9', 'c84c9148-846d-58eb-aac1-cd75c49ad306', 'Army', 'enlisted', '79S', null, 'self_development', 'E-6', null, 'Staff Sergeant — Self-development', 'Chapter 6. MOS 79S Career Counselor', 'All Career Counselors should strive to maximize every opportunity to pursue their civilian education that focuses on improving as a Career Counselor both personally and professionally. The Career Counselor’s willingness to pursue educational goals that enhance their job performance will assist in their future development. Continuing civilian education is indicative of good time management skills, self-development, and motivation. Associate’s degree or 60 semester hours towards a bachelor’s degree is preferred and considered a plus for promotion to Sergeant First Class. Winning the Secretary of the Army Career Counselor of the Year, ACOM ASCC/DRU Career Counselor of the Year, Division or Installation Career Counselor of the Year awards is indicative of a Career Counselors technical and tactical knowledge and is considered ahead of peers for promotion to Sergeant First Class. Recognitions such as SGT Audie Murphy and SGT Morales clubs, NCO of the Quarter/Year, and Instructor of the Quarter/Year, are also considered favorably by evaluation boards. NCOs will remain physically fit and always present an exceptional appearance. Those who score a 450 or above, with a score of 90 in each event on the AFT are clearly ahead of their peers and should be considered most qualified for promotion. Those who score a 400 or above, with a score of 80 in each event on the AFT are considered highly qualified for promotion.', '78aa9452c81026bdd134a495ddfa03d4058e5d60682f3eb90a23b64857ed2ee3', 20, 20, null, 59, null, null, false, null),
  ('97a1972c-a9dc-5e56-8481-dbcc6ea532f3', 'c84c9148-846d-58eb-aac1-cd75c49ad306', 'Army', 'enlisted', '79S', null, 'training', 'E-6', null, 'Staff Sergeant — Additional Training', 'Chapter 6. MOS 79S Career Counselor', 'Battle Staff NCO, Instructor Course, Master Resilience Trainer, Senior Retention Operations Course.', '267abea5727848ad1c9d5523142307c61c48fe8cab4f0db6a76066b824e1d89e', 20, 20, null, 60, null, null, false, null),
  ('201f0d33-ae09-53af-818a-3c42c4408bb5', 'c84c9148-846d-58eb-aac1-cd75c49ad306', 'Army', 'enlisted', '79S', null, 'assignments', 'E-6', null, 'Staff Sergeant — Special Assignments', 'Chapter 6. MOS 79S Career Counselor', 'Career Counselors should seek out special assignments only after successful completion of an operational assignment. This ensures the NCO acquires the knowledge, experience, and leadership abilities necessary to excel in the extremely limited positions that directly impact the 79S field. Examples of these generating and broadening assignments include HRC Analyst, Retention Operations NCO, Recruiter (SQI 4), and Drill Sergeant (SQI X). NCOs should not seek consecutive back-to-back special assignments.', 'e374435e76b4134fd28da0d51cad64d5144d1058eea165b179cc15296178ac38', 20, 20, null, 61, null, null, false, null),
  ('7d684e5b-cc27-5d0a-b446-14a08959e339', 'c84c9148-846d-58eb-aac1-cd75c49ad306', 'Army', 'enlisted', '79S', null, 'development', 'E-6', null, 'Staff Sergeant — COOL Web site', 'Chapter 6. MOS 79S Career Counselor', 'Seeking industry credentials is an important part of staying competitive within the MOS not only for documented continuing education and development, but to also stay current with industry level technical skills and trends. Earning the Society for Human Resources-Certified Professional (SHRM-CP) credential makes you most qualified for promotion. Completed credentials (i.e. USMAP, Lean Six Sigma, etc.), makes you highly qualified for promotion.', '68d22bdc8ca92574c54cf3016a61fffd017a852b23f61d08fd3dadbce2d30a53', 20, 20, null, 62, null, null, false, null),
  ('a42cc60d-a5ae-5b85-b139-90f33bc684aa', 'c84c9148-846d-58eb-aac1-cd75c49ad306', 'Army', 'enlisted', '79S', null, 'training', 'E-7', null, 'Sergeant First Class — Institutional training', 'Chapter 6. MOS 79S Career Counselor', 'Senior Leaders Course (SLC). Consider NCOs ahead of their peers and best qualified for promotion if they achieve distinguished recognition such as Distinguished Honor Graduate, Distinguished Leadership or Commandant’s List.', '1a288476c14a6f71a6ca9cae6fe2cb60a11dd6539221e11cfab3b0c27d7662c2', 20, 20, null, 63, null, null, false, null),
  ('5619359d-3585-53da-b912-8c19ae2b603e', 'c84c9148-846d-58eb-aac1-cd75c49ad306', 'Army', 'enlisted', '79S', null, 'assignments', 'E-7', null, 'Sergeant First Class — Operational Assignments', 'Chapter 6. MOS 79S Career Counselor', 'The focus during this phase of a Career Counselor’s career will be applying their technical skills and leadership abilities as the subject matter expert on the Army’s retention program while simultaneously fostering the development of less experienced Career Counselors. These NCOs will typically serve as the Career Counselor for a battalion sized element or higher, a smaller population that is geographically dispersed, or a stand-alone unit that is structured without a normal hierarchy in respects to retention support. Other operational assignments at this phase focus on leadership and management skills such as Senior Career Counselor and Senior Retention Operations NCO. The Senior Career Counselor serves as the technical advisor to the Commander at the Brigade or Division level on all aspects of the retention program while also directly supervising, developing, and providing support to subordinate Career Counselors. The Senior Retention Operations NCO serves in support of a Command Career Counselor at the Division, Corp, or ACOM level for retention processing and quality assurance. They implement and supervise local retention program procedures while exercising supervision over subordinate Retention Operations NCOs. They both implement and supervise local retention program procedures. The best qualified Career Counselors are those that show diversity in ACOM, ASCC, DRU assignments while consistently rated (highly qualified) as a Senior Career Counselor or Senior Retention Operations NCO. Assignments listed in (e) special assignments enhance career development and are considered highly demanding positions reserved for the best qualified Career Counselors after successful completion of an operational assignment. Successful performance in Skill Level 5 positions such as Senior Career Counselor or Senior Retention Operations NCO is a strong indication of potential success at the next skill level.', '35f0795d0883e03c10c70e874c4e86269614e3471e3e3fa5bdb953fae9ff0044', 21, 21, null, 64, null, null, false, null),
  ('0033d4dc-f329-58bb-83b7-62687eef07bf', 'c84c9148-846d-58eb-aac1-cd75c49ad306', 'Army', 'enlisted', '79S', null, 'self_development', 'E-7', null, 'Sergeant First Class — Self-development', 'Chapter 6. MOS 79S Career Counselor', 'All Career Counselors should strive to maximize every opportunity to pursue their civilian education that focuses on improving as a Career Counselor both personally and professionally. The Career Counselor’s willingness to pursue educational goals that enhance their job performance could assist in their future development. Courses in Statistics, Management, Communications, Marketing, Sales, and Human Resources will only enhance the Career Counselor’s ability to counsel and lead Soldiers. Continuing education is indicative of good time management skills, self-development, and motivation. Associate’s degree with an additional 30 semester or 90 semester hours towards a bachelor’s degree or higher is preferred and considered ahead of peers for promotion to Master Sergeant. Winning the Secretary of the Army Career Counselor of the Year, Retention Excellence Award in Leadership(REAL), James R. Bragg Senior Army Career Counselor of the Year, ACOM, ASCC/DRU Career Counselor of the Year, Division or Installation Career Counselor of the Year awards is indicative of a Career Counselors technical and tactical knowledge and is considered ahead of peers for promotion to Master Sergeant. Recognitions such as inductions into the SGT Audie Murphy and SGT Morales clubs, NCO of the Quarter/Year, and Instructor of the Quarter/Year, are also considered favorably by evaluation boards. NCOs will remain physically fit and always present an exceptional appearance. Those who score a 450 or above, with a score of 90 in each event on the AFT are clearly ahead of their peers and should be considered most qualified for promotion. NCOs who score 400 or above, with a score of 80 in each event on the AFT considered highly qualified for promotion.', 'a79c69eac016f781086179372a85ced811aa718768900f33a971640967a7e405', 21, 22, null, 65, null, null, false, null),
  ('7953f526-3cc2-58da-8d33-99aa97208cbf', 'c84c9148-846d-58eb-aac1-cd75c49ad306', 'Army', 'enlisted', '79S', null, 'training', 'E-7', null, 'Sergeant First Class — Additional Training', 'Chapter 6. MOS 79S Career Counselor', 'Battle Staff NCO, Instructor Course, Master Resilience Trainer, Senior Retention Operations Course.', '267abea5727848ad1c9d5523142307c61c48fe8cab4f0db6a76066b824e1d89e', 22, 22, null, 66, null, null, false, null),
  ('ca8bacff-4438-513b-a043-12bc26ae5189', 'c84c9148-846d-58eb-aac1-cd75c49ad306', 'Army', 'enlisted', '79S', null, 'assignments', 'E-7', null, 'Sergeant First Class — Special Assignments', 'Chapter 6. MOS 79S Career Counselor', 'Career Counselors should seek out special assignments only after successful completion of an operational assignment. This ensures the NCO acquires the knowledge, experience, and leadership abilities necessary to excel in the extremely limited positions that directly impact the 79S field. Examples of these generating and broadening assignments include Instructor/Writer , HRC Analyst, Small Group Leader, Retention Operations NCO, Trainer/Developer, Senior Army retention Operations NCO, Recruiter (ASI 4), Drill Sergeant (SQI X). . NCOs should not seek consecutive back-to-back special assignments.', 'b744d20bed6a27619f44b8246d29f85bea30beca2c29c1274cea8b1385f64776', 22, 22, null, 67, null, null, false, null),
  ('3ce35543-c978-542c-9f7f-58a2f07e71da', 'c84c9148-846d-58eb-aac1-cd75c49ad306', 'Army', 'enlisted', '79S', null, 'development', 'E-7', null, 'Sergeant First Class — COOL Web site', 'Chapter 6. MOS 79S Career Counselor', 'Seeking industry credentials is an important part of staying competitive within the MOS not only for documented continuing education and development, but to also stay current with industry level technical skills and trends. Earning the Society for Human Resources-Certified Professional (SHRM-CP) credential makes you most qualified for promotion. Completed credentials (i.e. USMAP, Lean Six Sigma, etc.), makes you highly qualified for promotion.', '68d22bdc8ca92574c54cf3016a61fffd017a852b23f61d08fd3dadbce2d30a53', 22, 22, null, 68, null, null, false, null),
  ('71e5b61c-d03b-5381-a003-37100089066b', 'c84c9148-846d-58eb-aac1-cd75c49ad306', 'Army', 'enlisted', '79S', null, 'training', 'E-8', null, 'Master Sergeant — Institutional training', 'Chapter 6. MOS 79S Career Counselor', 'MLC, US Army Sergeants Major Course (SGM-A).', 'e3f6a423c90e72e5bfc21e302f83e0a6857e62ab161c719478f74c260f9395f6', 22, 22, null, 69, null, null, false, null),
  ('e4f05de9-cad4-5acc-b714-4532945f0394', 'c84c9148-846d-58eb-aac1-cd75c49ad306', 'Army', 'enlisted', '79S', null, 'assignments', 'E-8', null, 'Master Sergeant — Operational Assignments', 'Chapter 6. MOS 79S Career Counselor', 'The focus during this phase of the Career Counselor’s career is to continue assignment diversity. It is encouraged and highly recommended that a Career Counselor seek opportunities to become well rounded at all levels of command. A Career Counselor will need to gain experience from diversification to reach the level necessary to become a Command Career Counselor. The most demanding operational positions in which a MSG can serve include Command Career Counselor, Senior Career Counselor, and Senior Retention Operations NCO. The Command Career Counselor’s primary function is to administer the daily operation of the Commanding General’s retention program. They assign an annual mission, exercise operational supervision, and enforce Army policy, directives, guidance, and processing procedures for their subordinate Senior Career Counselors and retention programs. The Senior Career Counselor’s job as the senior technical advisor to the commander is essential in the maintenance of unit combat readiness, Army end strength, and to supervise subordinate Career Counselors. The Senior Retention Operations NCO serves in support of a Command Career Counselor at the Division, Corp, or ACOM level for retention processing and quality assurance. They implement and supervise local retention program procedures while exercising supervision over subordinate Retention Operations NCOs. The best qualified Career Counselors are those that show diversity in ACOM, ASCC, DRU assignments while consistently rated (highly qualified) as a Command Career Counselor, Senior Career Counselor Senior Retention Operations NCO Assignments listed in (e) special assignments enhance career development and are considered demanding positions reserved for the best qualified Career Counselors after successful completion of an operational assignment. Successful performance in Skill Level 6 positions such as Command Career Counselor is a strong indication of potential success at the next skill level.', '3002f24968b21e6a168d3e4252aad894f7731c193ee980267b21d5c59ca957e7', 22, 23, null, 70, null, null, false, null),
  ('0cbec9fb-15ef-563f-b07c-f64891c702e0', 'c84c9148-846d-58eb-aac1-cd75c49ad306', 'Army', 'enlisted', '79S', null, 'self_development', 'E-8', null, 'Master Sergeant — Self-development', 'Chapter 6. MOS 79S Career Counselor', 'Continuing civilian education and completing a bachelor’s degree or higher is considered best qualified and will only enhance a Senior Career Counselor’s technical and professional knowledge. Winning the Retention Excellence Award in Leadership(REAL) or the James R. Bragg Senior Army Career Counselor of the Year is indicative of a Career Counselors technical and tactical knowledge and is considered ahead of peers for promotion to Sergeant Major. NCOs will remain physically fit and always present an exceptional appearance. Those who score a 450 or above, with a score of 90 in each event on the AFT are clearly ahead of their peers and should be considered most qualified for promotion. NCOs who score 400 or above, with a score of 80 in each event on the AFT are considered highly qualified for promotion.', 'e0cb9c2d0bc85385588567b47ebcc79ed75f08bacf425a483b4d9f69fa7755ab', 23, 23, null, 71, null, null, false, null),
  ('044fa873-5431-565d-96ba-75a0506748f9', 'c84c9148-846d-58eb-aac1-cd75c49ad306', 'Army', 'enlisted', '79S', null, 'training', 'E-8', null, 'Master Sergeant — Additional Training', 'Chapter 6. MOS 79S Career Counselor', 'Battle Staff NCO, Instructor Course, Master Resilience Trainer, Senior Retention Operations Course.', '267abea5727848ad1c9d5523142307c61c48fe8cab4f0db6a76066b824e1d89e', 23, 23, null, 72, null, null, false, null),
  ('d2ad1f78-64da-5f6a-9006-556abba44d01', 'c84c9148-846d-58eb-aac1-cd75c49ad306', 'Army', 'enlisted', '79S', null, 'assignments', 'E-8', null, 'Master Sergeant — Special Assignments', 'Chapter 6. MOS 79S Career Counselor', 'Career Counselors should seek out special assignments only after successful completion of an operational assignment. This ensures the NCO acquires the knowledge, experience, and leadership abilities necessary to excel in the extremely limited positions that directly impact the 79S field. Examples of these generating and broadening assignments include Senior Army Retention Operations NCO, Senior Career Management NCO, Talent Management NCO, Senior HRC Analyst, Chief Instructor, Senior Trainer/Developer, and RETAIN NCOIC. NCOs should not seek consecutive back-to-back special assignments.', 'c783e84c23f68765759f1029f95584eb813cc99da008031c4a29bf41b3141d4d', 23, 23, null, 73, null, null, false, null),
  ('126f0125-e4ce-515f-aaa9-c0c3c038dfbe', 'c84c9148-846d-58eb-aac1-cd75c49ad306', 'Army', 'enlisted', '79S', null, 'development', 'E-8', null, 'Master Sergeant — COOL Web site', 'Chapter 6. MOS 79S Career Counselor', 'Seeking industry credentials is an important part of staying competitive within the MOS not only for documented continuing education and development, but to also stay current with industry level technical skills and trends. Earning the Society for Human Resources-Certified Professional (SHRM-CP) credential makes you most qualified for promotion. Completed credentials (i.e. USMAP, Lean Six Sigma, etc.), makes you highly qualified for promotion.', '68d22bdc8ca92574c54cf3016a61fffd017a852b23f61d08fd3dadbce2d30a53', 23, 23, null, 74, null, null, false, null),
  ('a42fe259-3e7b-5ff8-bb88-61933ce6b8dc', 'c84c9148-846d-58eb-aac1-cd75c49ad306', 'Army', 'enlisted', '79S', null, 'training', 'E-9', null, 'Sergeant Major — Institutional training', 'Chapter 6. MOS 79S Career Counselor', 'US Army Sergeants Major Academy (SGM-A) Course.', '20219b0d42b97694035c20718461c51a8f305d009bc495f9e78c9bd6b1bedb55', 23, 23, null, 75, null, null, false, null),
  ('54e5bcc3-fa3e-5b08-ae0d-b3ebc81a7e8d', 'c84c9148-846d-58eb-aac1-cd75c49ad306', 'Army', 'enlisted', '79S', null, 'assignments', 'E-9', null, 'Sergeant Major — Operational Assignments', 'Chapter 6. MOS 79S Career Counselor', 'The primary operational assignment for a SGM is Command Career Counselor. The Command Career Counselor’s primary function is to administer the daily operation of the Commanding General’s retention program. They assign an annual mission, exercise operational supervision, and enforce Army policy, directives, guidance, and processing procedures for their subordinate Senior Career Counselors and retention programs. Command Career Counselors are normally assigned to progressively higher commands starting at the division or installation level. The Senior Command Career Counselor has a similar function. The main distinction is that a Senior Command Career Counselor has subordinate Command Career Counselors. These positions are commonly found at the corps or ACOM level and have a much larger area of responsibility along with a much wider sphere of influence. The best qualified Career Counselors are consistently rated (highly qualified) as a Command Career Counselor or Senior Command Career Counselor.', '5abacbb36e10e71bb57d083661cdc4334acfc2835539666c3a4ed97e89e0f202', 23, 24, null, 76, null, null, false, null),
  ('85cda140-f86b-5c84-8a0c-3ec64865e7c0', 'c84c9148-846d-58eb-aac1-cd75c49ad306', 'Army', 'enlisted', '79S', null, 'self_development', 'E-9', null, 'Sergeant Major — Self-development', 'Chapter 6. MOS 79S Career Counselor', 'At this stage continued self-development can only assist in the continued technical and professional development a SGM can provide to their subordinate Career Counselors. A bachelor’s degree is strongly recommended. Pursuit of a master’s degree is highly encouraged. NCOs will remain physically fit and always present an exceptional appearance. Those who score a 450 or above, with a score of 90 in each event on the AFT are clearly ahead of their peers and should be considered most qualified for promotion. NCOs who score 400 or above, with a score of 80 in each event on the AFT are considered highly qualified for promotion.', 'ae714edf817fafec2cf755cf2b63517501b5eca80f8dcb1d1205fc6269ba0ee0', 24, 24, null, 77, null, null, false, null),
  ('07a3e6b6-666d-58dc-9fc8-323c220cb641', 'c84c9148-846d-58eb-aac1-cd75c49ad306', 'Army', 'enlisted', '79S', null, 'training', 'E-9', null, 'Sergeant Major — Additional Training', 'Chapter 6. MOS 79S Career Counselor', 'Battle Staff NCO, Instructor Course, Master Resilience Trainer, Senior Retention Operations Course.', '267abea5727848ad1c9d5523142307c61c48fe8cab4f0db6a76066b824e1d89e', 24, 24, null, 78, null, null, false, null),
  ('d1aa0b25-ce39-52ae-87aa-80ed028cc52d', 'c84c9148-846d-58eb-aac1-cd75c49ad306', 'Army', 'enlisted', '79S', null, 'assignments', 'E-9', null, 'Sergeant Major — Special Assignments', 'Chapter 6. MOS 79S Career Counselor', 'Broadening assignments for a SGM include Director of the Retention Department, RRB SGM, Proponent SGM, and SGM-A Instructor.', 'e50ddc9192fca064f219193d1c7e9b3cfee4ce5beb29bfb1885125a53fd2e262', 24, 24, null, 79, null, null, false, null),
  ('e9c21f1a-914b-5c2b-8855-45f7351c71af', 'c84c9148-846d-58eb-aac1-cd75c49ad306', 'Army', 'enlisted', '79S', null, 'development', 'E-9', null, 'Sergeant Major — COOL Web site', 'Chapter 6. MOS 79S Career Counselor', 'Seeking industry credentials is an important part of staying competitive within the MOS not only for documented continuing education and development, but to also stay current with industry level technical skills and trends. Earning the Society for Human Resources-Certified Professional (SHRM-CP) credential makes you most qualified for promotion. Completed credentials (i.e. USMAP, Lean Six Sigma, etc.), makes you highly qualified for promotion.', '68d22bdc8ca92574c54cf3016a61fffd017a852b23f61d08fd3dadbce2d30a53', 24, 24, null, 80, null, null, false, null),
  ('974b6654-5d38-5a7e-993a-f61bad66445b', 'c84c9148-846d-58eb-aac1-cd75c49ad306', 'Army', 'enlisted', '79T', null, 'development', null, null, 'Overview', 'Chapter 8. MOS 79T Recruiting and Retention Noncommissioned', 'Officer (Army National Guard of the United States)', '9558b627c0cad96541603b8c26a6caa0a854b1308e71265fe50624e51d420442', 25, 25, null, 81, null, null, false, null),
  ('77a60d47-cd31-5aff-aee6-fda58e5e53fb', 'c84c9148-846d-58eb-aac1-cd75c49ad306', 'Army', 'enlisted', '79T', null, 'development', null, null, 'Goals for development', 'Chapter 8. MOS 79T Recruiting and Retention Noncommissioned', 'The purpose of the 79T PDM is to inform Non-Career Recruiters and Recruiting and Retention NCOs how their career and professional development patterns should transpire. All Soldiers seeking to career track MOS 79T must demonstrate proficiency as a Title 32 production recruiter for a minimum of 18 months to reclassify to MOS 79T IAW NGR 601-1. Only then can they convert to MOS 79T and seek Critical Development and broadening Title 32 or Title 10 assignments. Soldiers selected for positions other than a Title 32 RRNCO (production recruiting and retention NCO) will be seasoned and experienced in the 79T three tenet mission and hold at a minimum the ARNG Senior Recruiting and Retention Badge. Assignments must center on leadership positions at various levels. Soldiers should make every effort to seek the more challenging and professionally rewarding leadership positions such as serving as a First Sergeant or Command Sergeant Major. Those Soldiers who have diversified their careers and served in a myriad of key Leadership and Critical Development positions in the 79T MOS will be placed ahead of their peers during the evaluation board.', 'fefc6b6f9514d9fd2029298b235558bfe1093230886377e4f834e5e7df35043a', 28, 28, null, 82, null, null, false, null),
  ('f6b41697-a606-5f6f-a538-eb1fd777b78b', 'c84c9148-846d-58eb-aac1-cd75c49ad306', 'Army', 'enlisted', '79V', null, 'development', null, null, 'Goals for development', 'Chapter 10. MOS 79V Army Reserve Career Counselor', 'The purpose of the 79V PDM is to educate Career Counselors on how their careers and professional development (PD) patterns should unfold. This PDM will also depict the schooling, operational and special assignments, and self-development goals that define and enhance the qualifications needed for the career counselor to remain promotionally competitive, relevant, responsive, and ready to meet the needs of MOS 79V. NCOs will serve in several key and developmental positions throughout their career as they progress to develop tactical and technical expertise. These positions (or assignments) are separated in the two categories, operational and special. For the purpose of this chapter, MOS 79V defines operational assignments as a critical or key position. Special assignments are further separated into two additional sub-categories, Key Developmental assignments, and broadening assignments. Career counselors should make every effort to seek the more challenging and professionally rewarding leadership positions. BN and Group HQ level staff assignments are excellent for forming a well- rounded understanding of retention OPS and have a high OPTEMPO, but career counselors should only seek out these assignments after adequate development in 79V entry level assignments and leadership positions.', 'e5365604afc9ec4ba73fd7266332e76c3051ff1cffde9ee5371d0ed90dc9f098', 42, 42, null, 111, null, null, false, null),
  ('93ccbdc5-0a32-51c6-a5d3-3b923c20a35a', 'c84c9148-846d-58eb-aac1-cd75c49ad306', 'Army', 'enlisted', '79V', null, 'development', 'E-6', null, 'Staff Sergeant — overview', 'Chapter 10. MOS 79V Army Reserve Career Counselor', 'There are no authorized SSG positions for MOS 79V based on current force structure. However, Soldiers in the rank of SSG may fill valid vacant SFC 79V positions IAW Army manning guidance. SSGs assigned to SFC 79V positions must meet prerequisites for award of MOS based on DA Pam 611–21 and should refer to the guidance provided in Para (2) below as it relates to institutional', '5b701df52448e632c15a9214e497bc832bbcb2b4d900735adad90b8b5f9f7deb', 42, 42, null, 112, null, null, false, null),
  ('8e2eead3-8faf-5c9b-8a23-d3f859c5fdde', 'c84c9148-846d-58eb-aac1-cd75c49ad306', 'Army', 'enlisted', '79V', null, 'training', 'E-6', null, 'Staff Sergeant — training', 'Chapter 10. MOS 79V Army Reserve Career Counselor', 'training, operational assignments, self-development, additional training and special', '1d29bc366a4a3107b4d3af68ab5529f8622059bb4ad7455500edcf9b3e1f49b4', 42, 42, null, 113, null, null, false, null),
  ('32c18663-4d27-5b80-bda8-012915e3f1fe', 'c84c9148-846d-58eb-aac1-cd75c49ad306', 'Army', 'enlisted', '79V', null, 'training', 'E-7', null, 'Sergeant First Class — Institutional training', 'Chapter 10. MOS 79V Army Reserve Career Counselor', 'SLC. Consider NCOs ahead of their peers and best qualified for promotion if they achieve distinguished recognition such as Honor Graduate or Commandant’s List.', '0e98727292fcf3ebcba4cb3b10965978e669936c6b4b291d9b803195c8b20f7b', 42, 42, null, 114, null, null, false, null),
  ('67cd005d-a4a4-5e66-a987-5bfb4a71beda', 'c84c9148-846d-58eb-aac1-cd75c49ad306', 'Army', 'enlisted', '79V', null, 'assignments', 'E-7', null, 'Sergeant First Class — Operational assignments', 'Chapter 10. MOS 79V Army Reserve Career Counselor', 'The key leadership position and entry level assignment for the SFC is the ARCC. The focus during this phase of a Career Counselor’s career must be on continued development and refinement of their counseling and leadership skills in addition to their technical expertise. NCOs should successfully complete an assignment as an ARCC for at least 24 months before being considered for other operational assignments to be competitive for promotion to MSG. This time affords the NCO the opportunity to build a strong technical and tactical foundation for future assignments as a 79V and grounds the NCO in solid doctrinal procedures. Successful performance while serving in demanding leadership positions shows that an NCO has strong potential for advancement. ARCC’s stand out from their peers by accepting a variety of operational assignments in different geographical locations throughout their time as a 79V. Career Counselors should also focus on diversity by seeking out progressive key developmental and broadening assignments.
c. Self-development. Although the OPTEMPO is high, NCOs must take advantage of every opportunity to complete civilian education. A college degree is not a requirement, but it is strongly recommended that NCOs at this level pursue a Bachelor’s degree or equivalent. The purpose of this is to improve competitiveness and obtain a leadership edge. NCOs must demonstrate that they are dedicated to self-development and looking for better ways to lead. One way to accomplish this is to successfully complete college courses in areas such as management and communication. Soldiers who receive distinguished recognition as the Secretary of the Army ARCC of the Year, induction into the SGT Audie Murphy Club, Best Warrior Competition winner, or have been recognized as an honor graduate or commandants list for the ARCC course or 79V SLC, are clearly developing themselves at a level above their peers. NCOs will remain physically fit and always present an exceptional appearance. Those who score a 540 on the ACFT w/min 80 in each event or those with profiles score 90 points or above in each graded event are clearly ahead of their peers and should be considered most qualified for promotion. NCOs who score 480 on the ACFT w/min 80 in each event or those with profiles score 80 points or above in each graded event are considered highly qualified for promotion.
d. Additional training. Common Faculty Development-Instructor Course (CFD-IC) (SQI 8), Battle Staff OPS Course (ASI 2S), Transition NCO Course (ASI 4R), EOA Course (SQI Q), SHARP Course (ASI 1B), Inspector General Course (SQI B), and Holistic Health and Fitness Integrator (ASI P5).
e. Special assignments. The Army and the USAR provide a special opportunity for the most outstanding Soldiers to serve with distinction in both Key Developmental and broadening assignments. Key Developmental assignments include assignment as a BN or Group Senior NCO or Officer Accessions NCO. Broadening assignments. These positions should be considered equivalent to each other as they both require technical knowledge, leadership roles and duties. Reserve Component Career Counselor (RCCC) (ASI 4R), Senior Instructor (ASI 8), Command Retention Advisor (CRA) (formerly titled Command Career Counselor), HRC Talent Manager (TEAM 4), HRC Accession NCO, and Retention, Incentives & Policy Liaison LNO to USARC G-1. Successful performance in special assignments can be career enhancing. Another option available is to volunteer for special assignments. This alternative provides more control over when to accept an assignment and possibly the opportunity to select a choice location. Special assignments are in one tour increments and should not be performed consecutively to prevent loss of core MOS competencies and stagnating leadership potential. This practice enhances MOS proficiency and creates a well-rounded professional Soldier.
f. Army Career Degrees. See SOCAD Army Career Degree Program.
g. COOL Web site. Seeking industry credentials is an important part of staying competitive within the MOS not only for documented continuing education and development, but to also stay current with industry level technical skills and trends. Earning the Society for Human Resources-Certified Professional (SHRM-CP) credential makes you most qualified for promotion. Completed credentials (i.e. USMAP, Lean Six Sigma, etc.), makes you highly qualified for promotion.', '870d36e006050211b61fb4a9b4242bd3f6dc9e833d2ae214e26fe14180724fd4', 42, 44, null, 115, null, null, false, null),
  ('d33d5e2c-02c8-5247-9fc3-d4f7d01ed446', 'c84c9148-846d-58eb-aac1-cd75c49ad306', 'Army', 'enlisted', '79V', null, 'development', 'E-8', null, 'Master Sergeant — overview', 'Chapter 10. MOS 79V Army Reserve Career Counselor', '(a). Institutional training. Master Leader Course. Consider NCOs ahead of their peers and best qualified for promotion if they achieve distinguished recognition such as Honor Graduate or Commandant’s List.
(b). Operational assignments. The key leadership position and entry level assignment for the MSG is the Area Leader. MSGs should complete an assignment as an Areal Leader supervising at least 4 or more personnel for at least 24 months. 79V MSGs that perform successfully as an Area Leader should focus on diversity by seeking out progressive key developmental and broadening assignments. These assignments are in one-tour increments and should not be performed consecutively (that is within the same BN footprint or consecutive in another BN) to prevent loss of core MOS competencies and stagnating leadership potential. This practice enhances MOS proficiency and creates a well-rounded professional Soldier.
(c). Self-development. Continuing civilian education is beneficial to the development of the Sr. NCO. Civilian education is not a requirement to make SGM or for lateral appointment to CSM. However, continuing education (pursuit of a Master’s degree and/or Professional Certifications) is strongly recommended since promotion to SGM is very competitive and could make a difference between two equal records. Completion of a degree program demonstrates an ability to plan towards and attain specific self-development objectives.
(d) Additional training. Common Faculty Development-Instructor Course (CFD-FC) (SQI 8), Small Group Instructor Trainer Course (SGITC), Training Developer (SQI 2), Battle Staff OPS Course (ASI 2S), Transition NCO Course (ASI 4R), EOA Course (SQI Q), SHARP Course (ASI 1B), Inspector General Course (SQI B), and Holistic Health and Fitness Integrator (ASI P5). NCOs will remain physically fit and always present an exceptional appearance. Those who score a 540 on the ACFT w/min 80 in each event or those with profiles score 90 points or above in each graded event are clearly ahead of their peers and should be considered most qualified for promotion. NCOs who score 480 on the ACFT w/min 80 in each event or those with profiles score 80 points or above in each graded event are considered highly qualified for promotion.
(e) Special assignments. The Army and the USAR provide a special opportunity for outstanding Soldiers to serve with distinction in both Key Developmental and broadening assignments. Key Developmental assignments Senior Command Career Counselor, Senior Operations NCO, BN/Group Senior Officer Accessions NCO, Senior Sustainment NCO, Group Current Operations NCO/NCOIC, Group Senior IRR NCOIC, Group Senior Reenlistment Incentives and Policies NCOIC, Theatre Retention Manager. Broadening assignments include, Senior Instructor/Writer, Senior Training Developer/Writer, Senior RCCC Policy Manager, Senior Career Management NCO, Senior RCCC Operations NCO, nominative AGR 1SG positions, or detailed as an Assistant Inspector General. Successful performance in these assignments can be career enhancing. Special assignments are in one-tour increments and should not be performed consecutively to prevent loss of core MOS competencies and stagnating leadership potential. This practice enhances MOS proficiency and creates a well- rounded professional Soldier.
(f) Army Career Degrees. See SOCAD Army Career Degree Program.
(g) COOL Web site. Seeking industry credentials is an important part of staying competitive within the MOS not only for documented continuing education and development, but to also stay current with industry level technical skills and trends. Earning the Society for Human Resources-Certified Professional (SHRM-CP) credential makes you most qualified for promotion. Completed credentials (i.e. USMAP, Lean Six Sigma, etc.), makes you highly qualified for promotion.', 'aaa0f2dfec0414aa59f897c06271a875bbf0a4e8c4cdc213fdd7b548c0942a69', 44, 45, null, 116, null, null, false, null),
  ('21c9441b-e11b-501c-9ef4-75117e1c8cd2', 'c84c9148-846d-58eb-aac1-cd75c49ad306', 'Army', 'enlisted', '79V', null, 'development', 'E-9', null, 'Sergeant major/command sergeant major — overview', 'Chapter 10. MOS 79V Army Reserve Career Counselor', '(a). Institutional training. U.S. Army Sergeants Major Course.
(b). Operational assignments. The key leadership position and entry level assignment for the SGM is the BN SGM (ASI 6S). Progressive operational assignments include the ARCG Group OPS SGM (ASI 7S).
(c) Self-development. A master’s degree is recommended. NCOs will remain physically fit and always present an exceptional appearance. Those who score a 540 on the ACFT w/min 80 in each event or those with profiles score 90 points or above in each graded event are clearly ahead of their peers and should be considered most qualified for promotion. NCOs who score 480 on the ACFT w/min 80 in each event or those with profiles score 80 points or above in each graded event are considered highly qualified for promotion.
(d) Additional training. Battle Staff OPS Course (ASI 2S), Transition NCO Course (ASI 4R), Force Management Course (ASI A3), BN/Group Pre-Command Course, Command Legal Course.
(e) Special assignments. The Army and the USAR provide a special opportunity for outstanding Soldiers to serve with distinction in both Key Developmental and broadening assignments. Key Developmental assignments include ARCG Group OPS SGM (ASI 7S) and Senior ARCC (Army G-1 (ASI 7S). Broadening assignments include the USARCG CSM (ASI 7C), USARCG Group OPS SGM (ASI 7S) and Senior ARCC (Army G-1 (ASI 7S) RCCC SGM (ASI 7S), and USASMA SGM course instructor (ASI 7S).
(f) Army Career Degrees. See SOCAD Army Career Degree Program.
(g) COOL Web site. Seeking industry credentials is an important part of staying competitive within the MOS not only for documented continuing education and development, but to also stay current with industry level technical skills and trends. Earning the Society for Human Resources-Certified Professional (SHRM-CP) credential makes you most qualified for promotion. Completed credentials (i.e. USMAP, Lean Six Sigma, etc.), makes you highly qualified for promotion.', '375a67bbdcdb75a4fcb1c748368902ec708f865635c95a102e39b70a231ad4f6', 45, 46, null, 117, null, null, false, null);
commit;
